# Valgrind/Memcheck suppressions for the constant-time taint check (CTValgrind).
#
# PURPOSE: silence a small number of benign uninitialised-value reports that come
# from the FPC runtime (RTL), NOT from the crypto under test, so a clean subject
# run is not drowned in RTL noise.
#
# RULES (do not break these):
#   * Suppress ONLY frames in the FPC RTL / libc startup. NEVER suppress anything
#     in a Clp* crypto unit - that is exactly what the tool is meant to catch.
#   * NARROW EXCEPTION - public-output declassification: a branch inside a Clp*
#     unit MAY be suppressed IFF its condition is a deterministic function of a
#     value the routine is about to PUBLISH (the signature R, the public key A) -
#     i.e. public-equivalent, so branching on it leaks nothing beyond the output.
#     Such an entry MUST (a) be scoped to the single top frame, (b) carry a proof
#     comment of public-equivalence, (c) keep the underlying check in the code,
#     (d) never apply to a non-published intermediate. This is declassification,
#     not leak-hiding. Every other Clp* frame stays forbidden.
#   * After adding suppressions, re-run the CONTROLS (aes-ttable, ghash-4k) WITH
#     this file applied and confirm they STILL report an error. If a suppression
#     silences a control, it is too broad - remove it.
#
# HOW TO POPULATE (on first run, on a box where Valgrind starts - see the runbook):
#   1. Run a clean SUBJECT with generation on:
#        valgrind --error-exitcode=1 --gen-suppressions=all ./CTValgrind x25519
#   2. For each reported block that is clearly in the FPC RTL (fpc_*, SYSTEM_*,
#      FPC_* frames, or the libc/ld startup), paste the generated { ... } block
#      below and give it a descriptive name.
#   3. Re-run the controls with --suppressions=ct.supp and confirm they still fire.
#
# No suppressions are enabled by default: an empty file masks nothing, so it can
# never hide a real leak. Fill it in only with verified RTL frames.
#
# ---- Example shape (COMMENTED OUT - replace obj/fun with real RTL frames) ----
# {
#   fpc-rtl-startup-uninit
#   Memcheck:Cond
#   ...
#   fun:FPC_*
#   obj:*/CTValgrind
# }

# ---- Public-output declassification (see the NARROW EXCEPTION rule above) ----
# Ed25519/Ed448 ScalarMultBaseEncoded validates [k]B (k secret-derived) with
# CheckPoint before encoding it as R / the public key A. The branch is on the
# validity of a point that is about to be published -> public-equivalent; and its
# outcome is invariant ([k]B is a prime-order base-point multiple, hence always a
# valid non-identity point, so it never raises). The check is KEPT in the code
# (random-fault / DFA defence); only the ctgrind flag on this one public-equivalent
# branch is masked. Scoped to the top frame so no other branch is affected.
{
   ed25519-sign-point-validity-public-equivalent
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:CLPED25519$_$TED25519_$__$$_SCALARMULTBASEENCODED$*
}
{
   ed448-sign-point-validity-public-equivalent
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:CLPED448$_$TED448_$__$$_SCALARMULTBASEENCODED$*
}
